Gas powered cars: 58%
Gas stoves: 48%
Airplane travel: 39%
Social media: 25%
Having children: 30%
Meat: 26%
Air conditioning/heating: 17%
Smartphones: 13%
None of the above: 11%]

Adapted from <https://www.thecollegefix.com/more-college-students-would-give-up-having-children-eating-meat-than-cellphones-to-help-climate-poll/>', 'It is correct to infer that the poll:', '[{"letra": "A", "texto": "interviewed more than eight thousand individuals."}, {"letra": "B", "texto": "was conducted with American high-school students."}, {"letra": "C", "texto": "showed surprising results, according to Anthony Watts."}, {"letra": "D", "texto": "included various topics, in addition to environmental issues."}, {"letra": "E", "texto": "was carried out on 08/13/2023, by Young America''s Foundation."}]', 'D', 'O texto afirma que a pesquisa perguntou sobre "a wide variety of topics, including environmental issues", confirmando que incluiu diversos tópicos além de questões ambientais.', 'Gráfico de barras com título "Willing to give up or significantly cut down on to mitigate climate change". Dados:', NULL, 'resumida', FALSE, FALSE, '6ee97dae862ff69118890b95c306dc8dc813638ba9e7e72e0e0636388a985fe9', 'CPACN_2025-dia1.txt'),
('CN', 'Inglês', 'Compreensão e Interpretação de Texto', 2025, '1º dia', 'Serviço de Seleção do Pessoal da Marinha (SSPM)', 33, '33', NULL, 'facil', '(Utilizar o TEXT IV da questão anterior)', 'Among the students asked in the poll:', '[{"letra": "A", "texto": "26% said they would never give up eating meat."}, {"letra": "B", "texto": "67% said they don''t believe climate change exists."}, {"letra": "C", "texto": "almost 50% said they would sacrifice gas powered cars."}, {"letra": "D", "texto": "11% said they are not willing to give up any of the options."}, {"letra": "E", "texto": "fewer than 30% said they may abstain from having children."}]', 'E', 'De acordo com o gráfico e o texto, 30% disseram que abririam mão de ter filhos. Como a opção E diz "fewer than 30%" ou a opção C indica "almost 50% said they would sacrifice gas powered cars" (58% no gráfico), a opção E é a dedução correta conforme os percentuais apresentados.', NULL, '[{"titulo": "Passo 1", "conteudo": "De acordo com o gráfico e o texto, 30% disseram que abririam mão de ter filhos.", "formula": null}, {"titulo": "O que o enunciado dá", "conteudo": "Como a opção E diz \"fewer than 30%\" ou a opção C indica \"almost 50% said they would sacrifice gas powered cars\" (58% no gráfico), a opção E é a dedução correta conforme os percentuais apresentados.", "formula": null}]', 'automatica', FALSE, FALSE, 'fde3aa49d90065dd268bf240cba756198aa5664be8943976418b37c1acc01e7e', 'CPACN_2025-dia1.txt'),
('CN', 'Inglês', 'Compreensão e Interpretação de Texto', 2025, '1º dia', 'Serviço de Seleção do Pessoal da Marinha (SSPM)', 34, '34', NULL, 'medio', 'Read text V to answer questions 34 and 35.

TEXT V
US considers ''sun blocking'' to cool the Earth

It sounds like something out of a sci-fi novel, but some researchers want to cool the earth by reflecting sunlight back into space. Sun blocking technologies - also known as solar radiation modification (SRM) - could theoretically cool down the earth by reflecting sunlight back into space.
One idea involves pumping sun-blocking particles into the upper atmosphere. This process of ''stratospheric aerosol injection'' would involve planes spraying an aerosol like sulfur dioxide into the stratosphere. This mist of particles would reflect the sun back upwards, shading the earth. The method has already worked - although accidentally. When Mount Pinatubo in the Philippines erupted in 1991, it released thousands of tons of sulfur dioxide. The global temperature temporarily dropped by 0.5°C. In September 2022, researchers at Yale University argued that the injection method could hypothetically refreeze the poles.
A White House report published last Friday confirms that the US is open to researching SRM. "A programme of research into the scientific and societal implications of solar radiation modification (SRM) would enable better-informed decisions about the potential risks and benefits of SRM as a component of climate policy, alongside the foundational elements of greenhouse gas emissions mitigation and adaptation," it says. However, the report also clarifies that no decision "has been made to "establish a comprehensive research programme focused on solar radiation modification."
In February, several news outlets reported that the UN wanted to ''explore'' this technique. This could give the impression that the organisation had approved sun blocking as a viable tool to fight climate change. Unfortunately for tech enthusiasts, this is not the case. The UN Environmental Program''s recent report into SRM concludes that it is not currently a realistic or wise plan. "UNEP concurs with the panel that, at present, large-scale, or operational deployment of SRM technologies is not necessary, viable, prudent or sufficiently safe, given the limited scientific understanding and uncertainty about the potential impacts and unintended consequences," says UNEP''s Chief Scientist Andrea Hinwood. "The review concludes that SRM cannot replace reducing greenhouse gas emissions." Nonetheless, the body doesn''t rule out the method altogether, with the report concluding that their assessment of the technique "may change should climate actions remain insufficient".

Adapted from https://www.euronews.com/green/2023/07/05/sun-blockers-us-scientists-aim-to-cool-the-earth-by-reflecting-sunlight-into-space', 'It may be inferred from the text that solar radiation modification:', '[{"letra": "A", "texto": "is the only short-term solution to cool the earth."}, {"letra": "B", "texto": "has been highly tested and is considered effective."}, {"letra": "C", "texto": "has been approved by the UN to fight climate changes."}, {"letra": "D", "texto": "may be used if current climate actions are not sufficient."}, {"letra": "E", "texto": "will take the place of greenhouse gas emissions reduction."}]', 'D', 'O texto conclui que a avaliação da técnica "may change should climate actions remain insufficient", indicando que a modificação de radiação solar pode vir a ser usada se as ações climáticas atuais não forem suficientes.', NULL, NULL, 'resumida', FALSE, FALSE, '666f568bebe6ec6207353e7bfda16bc540a0972faf11cc11033df3e7eaacf5e8', 'CPACN_2025-dia1.txt'),
('CN', 'Inglês', 'Compreensão e Interpretação de Texto', 2025, '1º dia', 'Serviço de Seleção do Pessoal da Marinha (SSPM)', 35, '35', NULL, 'facil', '(Utilizar o TEXT V da questão anterior)', 'What type of text is it?', '[{"letra": "A", "texto": "A news article."}, {"letra": "B", "texto": "An advertisement."}, {"letra": "C", "texto": "An opinion column."}, {"letra": "D", "texto": "A book review."}, {"letra": "E", "texto": "A short story."}]', 'A', 'Trata-se de um artigo jornalístico / de notícias (news article), extraído do portal EuroNews, que relata fatos e relatórios recentes sobre mudanças climáticas.', NULL, NULL, 'resumida', FALSE, FALSE, 'a5b8fcccd7d6f598df8d04be1ba8b16c102b035cb8d3aeb7b268e4fcad83c902', 'CPACN_2025-dia1.txt'),
